This document specifies the Ed301-EdDSA and X301 algorithms for the SSH
protocol, as implemented on the "ed301" branch of OpenSSH-portable.

STATUS: DRAFT. Experimental and not standardised. The names below are
private-use names in the "eirindeval.de" namespace (RFC 4251 section 6),
not IANA registrations. This profile is not interoperable with any other
SSH implementation.

The design mirrors the existing OpenSSH algorithms one-to-one:

  Ed25519 template                     Ed301 profile
  ----------------------------------   ----------------------------------
  ssh-ed25519                          ssh-ed301@eirindeval.de
  ssh-ed25519-cert-v01@openssh.com     ssh-ed301-cert-v01@eirindeval.de
  curve25519-sha256                    x301-sha512@eirindeval.de
  mlkem768x25519-sha256                mlkem1024x301-sha512@eirindeval.de
  ssh-mldsa44-ed25519                  ssh-mldsa65-ed301@eirindeval.de
  ssh-mldsa44-ed25519-cert             ssh-mldsa65-ed301-cert-v01@eirindeval.de

Structure, wire layout and processing rules follow the template. Wherever
possible this profile follows the RFCs for the two standardised Edwards
curves: [RFC8032] for EdDSA, [RFC8709] for ssh-ed25519/ssh-ed448 and
[RFC8731] for curve25519-sha256/curve448-sha512. Where Ed25519 and Ed448
differ, the Ed448 rules apply, because Ed301 uses the Ed448 domain
construction (always-present context) and its security level is closer to
Ed448 than to Ed25519 (SHA-512, as in curve448-sha512).
Parameters are chosen for the Ed301 security level (approximately 2^149
generic attack cost), not copied from the 128-bit template: SHA-512 instead
of SHA-256, ML-KEM-1024 instead of ML-KEM-768, ML-DSA-65 instead of ML-DSA-44.

0. References

[ED301]   Ed301-EdDSA v2 byte contract, specifications/Ed301-EdDSA-v2.md and
          specifications/CURRENT_PROFILE.md in
          https://github.com/ychromosome/ed301 (branch Testing, reviewed at
          commit 9ef3fc21e0f79331f1c885907cf2805f5d2698aa).
[X301]    X301 v2 byte contract, specifications/X301-v2.md, same source.
[RFC4251] SSH Protocol Architecture (data types, private-use names).
[RFC4252] SSH Authentication Protocol.
[RFC4253] SSH Transport Layer Protocol.
[RFC8032] Edwards-Curve Digital Signature Algorithm (EdDSA).
[RFC5656] ECC in SSH (SSH_MSG_KEX_ECDH_INIT / _REPLY, section 4).
[RFC8709] Ed25519 and Ed448 public key algorithms for SSH.
[RFC8731] Curve25519 and Curve448 key exchange for SSH.
[MLKEM-SSH] draft-ietf-sshm-mlkem-hybrid-kex (mlkem768x25519-sha256).
[COMPSIG-SSH] draft-miller-sshm-mldsa44-ed25519-composite-sigs-00.
[COMPSIG] draft-ietf-lamps-pq-composite-sigs-18.
[FIPS203] ML-KEM.  [FIPS204] ML-DSA.
PROTOCOL.key, PROTOCOL.sshsig and PROTOCOL.agent in this tree.

1. Ed301 parameters used by this profile

All values come from [ED301]:

  seed / private key material   38 bytes
  public key (compressed point) 38 bytes
  signature (R || S)            76 bytes
  signature context             binary string, 0..255 bytes

Signing is deterministic and one-shot ("pure" EdDSA, no prehash).
The Ed301 domain string is always present:
  dom(C) = "SigEd301-v2" || 0x00 || octet(len(C)) || C

1.1. SSH signature context

Every Ed301 signature made under this profile, except the composite
component in section 3, uses the empty context:

  C_SSH = "" (zero-length)

This is the [RFC8709] rule for ssh-ed448, which uses Ed448 (always-present
context, [RFC8032] section 5.2) with an empty context. The domain string
dom(C_SSH) is still included, as [ED301] requires. Separation from other
protocols comes from the structure of the signed SSH data (session
identifier, "SSHSIG" magic), as for ssh-ed25519 and ssh-ed448. It does not
come from the EdDSA context.

2. Public key algorithm "ssh-ed301@eirindeval.de"

2.1. Public key format (as [RFC8709] section 4)

  string  "ssh-ed301@eirindeval.de"
  string  key          38 bytes, compressed Ed301 point

A receiver MUST reject a key string whose length is not 38. Before using a
public key for verification, a receiver MUST apply the [ED301] public key
rules: canonical encoding, not the identity, and member of the prime-order
subgroup. An implementation MAY apply these checks when it parses the key.

2.2. Signature format (as [RFC8709] section 6)

  string  "ssh-ed301@eirindeval.de"
  string  signature    76 bytes, R || S as in [ED301]

A receiver MUST reject a signature blob with trailing data, a signature
string whose length is not 76, or an algorithm name that does not match.

2.3. Signing and verification

The signer computes Ed301-Sign(seed, M, C_SSH) over the exact data that
SSH supplies to the signature operation (for example the exchange hash H,
or the user authentication data of [RFC4252] section 7). No additional
hashing is done.

The verifier accepts exactly when Ed301-Verify(pk, M, sig, C_SSH)
succeeds under [ED301]. That includes its cofactored equation
[4S]G = [4]R + [4k]A and the canonical encoding rules for R and S.

2.4. Private key format

In the openssh-key-v1 container (PROTOCOL.key) and the agent protocol, the
key-type-specific private fields are (same layout as ssh-ed25519):

  string  pk           38 bytes
  string  sk           76 bytes = seed (38) || pk (38)

This matches the Ed25519 convention, where sk = seed (32) || pk (32). Only
the seed is secret. The loader MUST check that pk equals the public key
derived from the seed, and that the trailing 38 bytes of sk equal pk.
Signing with a mismatched public key would expose the EdDSA "double public
key" oracle. Ed301 signing always uses the public key derived from the seed.

2.5. Certificates

"ssh-ed301-cert-v01@eirindeval.de" uses the OpenSSH certificate format
exactly as "ssh-ed25519-cert-v01@openssh.com" does. The type-specific key
field is:

  string  pk           38 bytes

The CA signature on a certificate uses the CA key's own algorithm. An Ed301
CA uses the signature format in 2.2 with context C_SSH.

3. Composite public key algorithm "ssh-mldsa65-ed301@eirindeval.de"

This follows [COMPSIG-SSH] and [COMPSIG] as implemented in
ssh-mldsa-eddsa.c, with ML-DSA-65 in place of ML-DSA-44 and Ed301 in place
of Ed25519.

3.1. Constants

  Prefix = ASCII "CompositeAlgorithmSignatures2025"
  Label  = ASCII "COMPSIG-MLDSA65-Ed301-SHA512"

  ML-DSA-65 public key 1952, signature 3309, seed 32 bytes [FIPS204]
  composite public key  = mldsa_pk (1952) || ed301_pk (38)    = 1990 bytes
  composite signature   = mldsa_sig (3309) || ed301_sig (76)  = 3385 bytes
  composite private key = mldsa_seed (32) || ed301_seed (38)  =   70 bytes

3.2. Formats

  Public key:  string "ssh-mldsa65-ed301@eirindeval.de", string pk (1990)
  Signature:   string "ssh-mldsa65-ed301@eirindeval.de", string sig (3385)
  Private key fields (non-certificate): string pk (1990), string sk (70)
  Private key fields (certificate):      string sk (70)

The private key layout follows ssh_mldsa44_ed25519_serialize_private().

3.3. Signing

For the SSH data M, with the composite context ctx set to empty (as in
the template):

  M' = Prefix || Label || octet(len(ctx)) || ctx || SHA-512(M)

  mldsa_sig = ML-DSA-65.Sign(mldsa_sk, M', ctx = Label)    hedged, [FIPS204]
  ed301_sig = Ed301-Sign(ed301_seed, M', C = Label)        [ED301]
  sig       = mldsa_sig || ed301_sig

Difference from the template: Ed25519 has no context input, so the
template signs M' with plain Ed25519. Ed301 does have a context, and it
receives Label, the same way ML-DSA does. A component Ed301 signature
therefore never verifies as a standalone "ssh-ed301@eirindeval.de"
signature (empty context C_SSH), which defeats component stripping.

3.4. Verification

The verifier recomputes M' and accepts only if BOTH components verify:
ML-DSA-65 under ctx = Label, and Ed301 under C = Label using the [ED301]
public key rules.

4. Key exchange "x301-sha512@eirindeval.de"

This is [RFC8731] with X301 substituted for X25519/X448. HASH is SHA-512,
as for curve448-sha512.
The messages are SSH_MSG_KEX_ECDH_INIT and SSH_MSG_KEX_ECDH_REPLY
([RFC5656] section 4):

  Client:  string Q_C   38 bytes, X301 public value
  Server:  string K_S, string Q_S (38 bytes), string signature of H

Ephemeral keys are generated by the [X301] KeyGen procedure. It draws 38
random bytes and resamples only on the excluded weak-secret condition.

Both sides compute the 38-byte output X301(own_secret, peer_Q) under [X301].
They MUST abort the key exchange if:
  - a received Q has a length other than 38;
  - a received Q is not canonical under [X301] (bits 301..303 set, or
    u >= p). Such a value is never masked or reduced;
  - the X301 operation fails, including a result at infinity or an
    all-zero output.

The 38 output bytes are interpreted as an unsigned integer in network byte
order and encoded as an mpint to form K, exactly as [RFC8731] section 3.1
does for X25519. The exchange hash H is computed with SHA-512 over the
fields of [RFC5656] section 4.

5. Key exchange "mlkem1024x301-sha512@eirindeval.de"

This is [MLKEM-SSH] with ML-KEM-1024 in place of ML-KEM-768, X301 in place
of X25519, and SHA-512 as HASH. The message flow and processing are
otherwise those of mlkem768x25519-sha256 as implemented in
kexmlkem768x25519.c.

5.1. Messages

  SSH_MSG_KEX_HYBRID_INIT (client):
    string C_INIT = C_PK2 || C_PK1
       C_PK2  ML-KEM-1024 encapsulation key  1568 bytes
       C_PK1  X301 public value                38 bytes   (total 1606)

  SSH_MSG_KEX_HYBRID_REPLY (server):
    string K_S
    string S_REPLY = S_CT2 || S_PK1
       S_CT2  ML-KEM-1024 ciphertext         1568 bytes
       S_PK1  X301 public value                38 bytes   (total 1606)
    string signature of H

ML-KEM comes first and the classical part second, as in the template.
This is the same component order and length as the project's TLS hybrid
group X301MLKEM1024 (NamedGroup 0xFE2F). The SSH combiner in 5.2 is
SSH-specific, however.

5.2. Shared secret

  K_PQ = ML-KEM-1024 shared secret      32 bytes
  K_CL = X301 output                    38 bytes, raw (no mpint)
  K    = SHA-512(K_PQ || K_CL)          64 bytes, encoded as string

The exchange hash H is computed with SHA-512. K is encoded as a string, not
an mpint, as in [MLKEM-SSH].

5.3. Checks

The server MUST reject C_INIT unless it has exactly 1606 bytes. It MUST
reject an encapsulation key that fails the [FIPS203] section 7.2 input
check (modulus check). The client MUST reject S_REPLY unless it has exactly
1606 bytes. The X301 abort conditions of section 4 apply to C_PK1 and
S_PK1. A failure in either component aborts the key exchange. No partial
or fallback secret is ever used.

5.4. ML-KEM implementation

ML-KEM-1024 is provided by the embedded libcrux extraction in both builds,
like ML-KEM-768 for mlkem768x25519-sha256. It is not taken from libcrypto.

6. Implementation notes (non-normative)

6.1. Backends

The cryptographic primitives sit behind crypto_api.h and the backend is
selected at build time:

  WITH_OPENSSL     ed301-openssl.c and x301-openssl.c use EVP algorithms
                   "Ed301-EdDSA" and "X301" in the default library context,
                   activated by the openssl-provider-ed301-policy package.
  !WITH_OPENSSL    --with-ed301-rust=DIR selects ed301-rust.c and the small
                   ed301-ssh-capi static library over the public Rust core
                   APIs of [ED301]/[X301]. Without this option Ed301/X301
                   are not compiled into a build without OpenSSL.

ML-DSA-65 and ML-KEM-1024 always come from embedded libcrux. The latter
has its own portable extraction translation unit, separate from the
existing ML-KEM-768/ML-DSA extraction. PKCS#8 for standalone Ed301 private
keys is supported only by the EVP backend and the provider encoders.

6.2. Runtime availability

In the EVP backend the algorithms are available only when the provider is
active in the default library context. Unlike Ed25519, availability is
therefore probed at run time. If the provider is missing, the algorithms
are removed from the offered KEX and host key algorithm lists and from
server-sig-algs, `ssh -Q` does not list them, and ssh-keygen reports a
clear error. Configured and default lists (`ssh -G`, `sshd -T`) stay
static like those of any other compiled algorithm; keys of these types
fail to load or verify and are skipped. Their absence is never a fatal
error for other algorithms.

6.3. Out of scope

  - FIDO/U2F variants (sk-ssh-ed25519@openssh.com): no authenticator
    implements Ed301.
  - PKCS#11: no token implements Ed301.
  - SSHFP DNS records: Ed301 has no IANA SSHFP algorithm number.

